# NASA Exoplanet Archive

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:space_tap, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19.1"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1.13"}
])
```

## The service

The [NASA Exoplanet Archive](https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu), run by IPAC at Caltech, keeps the catalogue of confirmed planets around other stars. Its table `pscomppars` has one row per planet, with the best value of each parameter.

The archive sends its CSV with `Content-Type: text/plain` instead of the `text/csv` that TAP 1.1 §3.2 requires. SpaceTAP reads it as CSV anyway, because CSV is what it asked for (`docs/adr/0003-csv-sent-as-plain-text-is-read-as-csv.md`).

```elixir
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

nasa = "https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu/TAP"

to_maps = fn %SpaceTAP.Result{columns: columns, rows: rows} ->
  Enum.map(rows, fn row -> columns |> Enum.zip(row) |> Map.new() end)
end

number = fn
  nil ->
    nil

  string ->
    {number, ""} = Float.parse(string)
    number
end
```

## Discoveries by method

ADQL has `GROUP BY` and `COUNT`, so the service does the counting:

```elixir
{:ok, by_method} =
  SpaceTAP.query(nasa, """
  SELECT discoverymethod, COUNT(*) AS planets
  FROM pscomppars
  GROUP BY discoverymethod
  ORDER BY planets DESC
  """)

Kino.DataTable.new(to_maps.(by_method), keys: by_method.columns, name: "Planets by method")
```

Most planets were found by the transit method, as they crossed in front of their star and dimmed it; Kepler and TESS found thousands that way. Radial velocity measures the wobble of the star as the planet pulls on it.

## Discoveries per year

```elixir
{:ok, by_year} =
  SpaceTAP.query(nasa, """
  SELECT disc_year, discoverymethod, COUNT(*) AS planets
  FROM pscomppars
  GROUP BY disc_year, discoverymethod
  """)

discoveries =
  for row <- to_maps.(by_year) do
    %{
      "year" => String.to_integer(row["disc_year"]),
      "method" => row["discoverymethod"],
      "planets" => String.to_integer(row["planets"])
    }
  end

Vl.new(width: 700, height: 350, title: "Exoplanets discovered per year")
|> Vl.data_from_values(discoveries)
|> Vl.mark(:bar)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "year", type: :ordinal, title: "Year")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "planets", type: :quantitative, aggregate: :sum, title: "Planets")
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "method", type: :nominal, title: "Method")
```

The two tallest bars, 2016 and 2014, are the years when large batches of Kepler planets were confirmed at once.

## Planets by size and orbit

Each dot is a planet: its orbital period, how long its year lasts, against its radius in Earth radii. Both axes are logarithmic, because periods go from hours to centuries:

```elixir
{:ok, sizes} =
  SpaceTAP.query(nasa, """
  SELECT pl_name, pl_orbper, pl_rade, discoverymethod
  FROM pscomppars
  WHERE pl_orbper IS NOT NULL AND pl_rade IS NOT NULL
  """)

planets =
  for row <- to_maps.(sizes) do
    %{
      "name" => row["pl_name"],
      "period_days" => number.(row["pl_orbper"]),
      "radius_earth" => number.(row["pl_rade"]),
      "method" => row["discoverymethod"]
    }
  end

Vl.new(width: 700, height: 450, title: "Orbital period and radius")
|> Vl.data_from_values(planets)
|> Vl.mark(:circle, size: 12, opacity: 0.5)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "period_days",
  type: :quantitative,
  title: "Period (days)",
  scale: [type: :log]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "radius_earth",
  type: :quantitative,
  title: "Radius (Earth = 1)",
  scale: [type: :log]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "method", type: :nominal, title: "Method")
|> Vl.encode_field(:tooltip, "name", type: :nominal)
```

The clump at the top left are hot Jupiters, giant planets that orbit their star in a few days. Most planets found by transit have short periods, because a planet has to cross its star while someone is watching.

## The nearest planets, and a quirk of the service

`sy_dist` is the distance to the planet's system in parsecs. In ADQL, `ORDER BY` sorts the rows before `TOP` keeps the first ones: ADQL 2.1 §4.10.1 applies `ORDER BY`, then `OFFSET`, then `TOP`. This service applies `TOP` first, so the query below returns ten planets that are not the nearest, sorted:

```elixir
{:ok, wrong} =
  SpaceTAP.query(nasa, """
  SELECT TOP 10 pl_name, sy_dist
  FROM pscomppars
  WHERE sy_dist IS NOT NULL
  ORDER BY sy_dist
  """)

wrong.rows
```

`MAXREC` is applied to the final result, so leave out `TOP` and pass `:maxrec` instead:

```elixir
{:ok, nearest} =
  SpaceTAP.query(
    nasa,
    """
    SELECT pl_name, hostname, sy_dist, disc_year, discoverymethod
    FROM pscomppars
    WHERE sy_dist IS NOT NULL
    ORDER BY sy_dist
    """,
    maxrec: 10
  )

Kino.DataTable.new(to_maps.(nearest), keys: nearest.columns, name: "Nearest planets")
```

Proxima Centauri, the nearest star to the Sun, has planets 1.3 parsecs away.

## Errors

The archive sends its error documents as VOTable, so they are `:service_error`s:

```elixir
SpaceTAP.query(nasa, "SELECT TOP 3 pl_name FROM no_such_table")
```
